When a stream starts on an open GOP (an H.264 recovery point, an HEVC CRA or
an MPEG-2 I-frame without closed_gop), the leading pictures reference a GOP
that was cut off. h264dec, hevcdec and mpeg12dec drop them. The AMF runtime
outputs them as concealed pictures and doesn't mark them, so the picture
started on garbage and late by as many frames.

Classify each packet from its headers before submission and carry that to
the decoded surface as buffer properties, as amfdec already does for dts. A
picture is a droppable leading picture if it is an HEVC RASL picture, an
MPEG-2 B-picture after an open GOP header, or an H.264 non-IDR picture when
the first keyframe has a recovery point SEI with recovery_frame_cnt 0 or,
with no recovery point SEI, contains only reference I-slices. A recovery
point with a nonzero count is not overridden. HEVC RADL pictures, closed or
unknown MPEG-2 GOPs and streams starting on an H.264 IDR are left alone.
Keyframes are tagged too, so the MPEG-2 keyframe isn't mistaken for a
leading B-picture with the same pts.

Until the first frame comes out after init or flush, drop droppable leading
pictures that display at or before the first keyframe, unless output_corrupt
or showall is set. Stop once any frame has been output: a stream that starts
before its first keyframe would otherwise lose the frames just before it and
keep the ones before those.

